<section class="chapter" id="programs-processes-intro">
<h1>Programs and Processes of Computer</h1>
<p>
        Programs and processes are fundamental concepts in
        computer science, operating systems, and software execution.
        A program is a passive collection of instructions stored on
        secondary storage, while a process is an active instance
        of a program executing under operating system control.
    </p>
<h3>Program vs. Process</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Feature</th>
<th>Program</th>
<th>Process</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Nature</td>
<td>Static / Passive</td>
<td>Dynamic / Active</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Storage</td>
<td>Secondary storage (SSD/HDD)</td>
<td>RAM and CPU execution context</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Lifespan</td>
<td>Remains stored until removed</td>
<td>Exists during execution</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Resources</td>
<td>Storage space</td>
<td>CPU, RAM, files, and I/O</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>OS Management</td>
<td>File system</td>
<td>Kernel scheduler and PCB</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Basic Computer Components</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Input Unit:</strong> Receives data and instructions.</li>
<li><strong>Output Unit:</strong> Presents processed results.</li>
<li><strong>Memory Unit:</strong> Stores actively used instructions and data.</li>
<li><strong>Arithmetic Logic Unit (ALU):</strong> Performs calculations and logical operations.</li>
<li><strong>Control Unit (CU):</strong> Coordinates instruction execution.</li>
<li><strong>Secondary Storage:</strong> Stores programs and data for long-term use.</li>
</ul>
<h3>How a Computer Executes Instructions</h3>
<ol>
<li>A programmer creates instructions and data.</li>
<li>The program is translated and stored on disk.</li>
<li>The operating system loads the executable into memory.</li>
<li>The CPU fetches an instruction using the program counter.</li>
<li>The control unit decodes the instruction.</li>
<li>The CPU obtains operands and executes the operation.</li>
<li>Results are stored and the next instruction is fetched.</li>
</ol>
<div class="code-example">
<pre><code>PROGRAM + DATA
      |
      v
SECONDARY STORAGE
      |
      v
OPERATING SYSTEM LOADER
      |
      v
PRIMARY MEMORY (RAM)
      |
      v
CPU: FETCH -&gt; DECODE -&gt; EXECUTE
      |
      v
OUTPUT / STORED RESULTS</code></pre>
</div>
<p>
        Computers represent programs and data using binary digits,
        or bits, consisting of 0 and 1. Eight bits form one byte.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="program-foundations">
<h2>Section 1: Foundational Definitions and Operational Paradigms</h2>
<h3>What Is a Program?</h3>
<p>
        A computer program is a collection of instructions written
        in a programming language such as C, C++, Rust, Go,
        Python, Java, or assembly language.
    </p>
<p>
        A program exists as a passive entity on non-volatile
        storage until the operating system initiates its execution.
    </p>
<h3>What Is a Process?</h3>
<p>
        A process is an active execution instance of a program.
        The operating system loads the executable, establishes
        a virtual address space, allocates resources, and
        assigns a Process ID (PID).
    </p>
<h3>Executable File Formats</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>ELF:</strong> Common executable format on Linux and UNIX-like systems.</li>
<li><strong>PE:</strong> Portable Executable format used by Windows.</li>
<li><strong>Mach-O:</strong> Executable format used by macOS.</li>
</ul>
<p>
        A single executable program may create multiple independent
        processes, each with its own execution state.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="compilation-linking">
<h2>Section 2: Compilation, Assembly, and Linking Pipeline</h2>
<p>
        Before source code can execute as a native program,
        it may pass through several translation stages.
    </p>
<h3>Four Stages of Binary Translation</h3>
<ol>
<li>
<strong>Preprocessing:</strong>
            Resolves directives such as #include and #define,
            expands macros, and prepares source code.
        </li>
<li>
<strong>Compilation:</strong>
            Translates source code into assembly instructions
            while performing syntax checks and optimization.
        </li>
<li>
<strong>Assembly:</strong>
            Converts assembly instructions into machine-code
            object files such as .o or .obj.
        </li>
<li>
<strong>Linking:</strong>
            Resolves references between object files and
            libraries to create an executable.
        </li>
</ol>
<div class="code-example">
<pre><code>SOURCE CODE
    |
    v
PREPROCESSOR
    |
    v
COMPILER
    |
    v
ASSEMBLER
    |
    v
OBJECT FILES
    |
    v
LINKER + LIBRARIES
    |
    v
EXECUTABLE PROGRAM
    |
    v
OS LOADER -&gt; RUNNING PROCESS</code></pre>
</div>
<h3>Static Linking vs. Dynamic Linking</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Static Linking</th>
<th>Dynamic Linking</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Copies library code into the executable.</td>
<td>Uses external shared libraries.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Generally increases executable size.</td>
<td>Can reduce executable size.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Fewer external runtime library dependencies.</td>
<td>Requires compatible shared libraries at runtime.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Executable Headers</h3>
<p>
        Executable headers contain information such as the
        target CPU architecture, entry point, segment mappings,
        and memory permissions.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="process-memory">
<h2>Section 3: Detailed Process Memory Architecture</h2>
<p>
        The operating system organizes each process into a
        virtual address space containing several memory regions.
    </p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Memory Segment</th>
<th>Purpose</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Text</td>
<td>Compiled executable instructions.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Data</td>
<td>Initialized global and static variables.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>BSS</td>
<td>Uninitialized global and static variables.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Heap</td>
<td>Dynamic memory allocations.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Stack</td>
<td>Function calls, local variables, and return addresses.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Conceptual Memory Layout</h3>
<div class="code-example">
<pre><code>HIGH MEMORY ADDRESSES
+--------------------------+
| Kernel Reserved Space    |
+--------------------------+
| Stack                    |
| Grows Downward           |
|           v              |
+--------------------------+
|                          |
|           ^              |
| Heap                     |
| Grows Upward             |
+--------------------------+
| BSS                      |
+--------------------------+
| Initialized Data         |
+--------------------------+
| Text / Program Code      |
+--------------------------+
LOW MEMORY ADDRESSES</code></pre>
</div>
<p>
        Dynamic allocation functions such as malloc() and
        new request heap memory. The stack follows a
        Last-In, First-Out (LIFO) organization.
    </p>
<p>
        Exceeding available stack space can cause
        a stack overflow.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="process-control-block">
<h2>Section 4: The Process Control Block (PCB)</h2>
<p>
        The Process Control Block is a kernel data structure
        that stores the information required to manage,
        interrupt, and resume a process.
    </p>
<h3>PCB Components</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Field</th>
<th>Description</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Process ID (PID)</td>
<td>Unique process identifier.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Process State</td>
<td>Current execution status.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Program Counter</td>
<td>Address of the next instruction.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CPU Registers</td>
<td>Saved processor values.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Scheduling Metadata</td>
<td>Priority and scheduling information.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Memory Information</td>
<td>Page tables and memory mappings.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>I/O Information</td>
<td>Open files and device handles.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Accounting</td>
<td>CPU time and execution statistics.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>
        The PCB is especially important during context switching,
        when the operating system saves and restores execution state.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="process-lifecycle">
<h2>Section 5: Process Lifecycle State Machine</h2>
<p>
        A process moves through several operating states
        during its lifetime.
    </p>
<h3>Five Fundamental Process States</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>New:</strong> The process is being created.</li>
<li><strong>Ready:</strong> Waiting for CPU assignment.</li>
<li><strong>Running:</strong> Instructions are executing.</li>
<li><strong>Waiting / Blocked:</strong> Waiting for an event or I/O completion.</li>
<li><strong>Terminated:</strong> Execution has finished.</li>
</ul>
<div class="code-example">
<pre><code>NEW ---&gt; READY ---&gt; RUNNING ---&gt; TERMINATED
           ^          |
           |          | I/O Request
           |          v
           +------- WAITING
             I/O Completed

RUNNING ---&gt; READY
  (Preemption / Time Slice Expired)</code></pre>
</div>
<p>
        The CPU scheduler dispatches ready processes.
        A blocked process returns to the ready queue
        when its required event is completed.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="cpu-scheduling-principles">
<h2>Section 6: CPU Scheduling Principles and Metrics</h2>
<p>
        CPU scheduling determines which ready process
        receives execution time on an available processor core.
    </p>
<h3>Preemptive Scheduling</h3>
<p>
        The operating system can interrupt a running process
        and assign the CPU to another process.
    </p>
<h3>Non-Preemptive Scheduling</h3>
<p>
        A running process keeps the CPU until it completes,
        blocks, or voluntarily releases control.
    </p>
<h3>Performance Metrics</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Metric</th>
<th>Meaning</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>CPU Utilization</td>
<td>Percentage of time the CPU is busy.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Throughput</td>
<td>Processes completed per unit time.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Turnaround Time</td>
<td>Time from submission to completion.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Waiting Time</td>
<td>Time spent in the ready queue.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Response Time</td>
<td>Time until the first response.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="cpu-scheduling-algorithms">
<h2>Section 7: In-Depth CPU Scheduling Algorithms</h2>
<h3>First-Come, First-Served (FCFS)</h3>
<p>
        Processes execute in arrival order.
        This simple method can cause the convoy effect,
        where short jobs wait behind long-running jobs.
    </p>
<h3>Shortest Job First (SJF)</h3>
<p>
        Selects the process with the shortest estimated
        CPU burst. It can reduce average waiting time.
    </p>
<h3>Shortest Remaining Time First (SRTF)</h3>
<p>
        A preemptive version of SJF that can interrupt
        a running process when a shorter job arrives.
    </p>
<h3>Priority Scheduling</h3>
<p>
        Processes are selected according to priority.
        Aging can reduce starvation by increasing
        the priority of waiting processes.
    </p>
<h3>Round-Robin Scheduling</h3>
<p>
        Each process receives a fixed time quantum.
        A process that does not finish is returned
        to the ready queue.
    </p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Algorithm</th>
<th>Advantage</th>
<th>Limitation</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>FCFS</td>
<td>Simple implementation</td>
<td>Convoy effect</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>SJF</td>
<td>Low average waiting time</td>
<td>Requires burst estimates</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>SRTF</td>
<td>Responds to shorter jobs</td>
<td>Preemption overhead</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Priority</td>
<td>Supports task importance</td>
<td>Possible starvation</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Round Robin</td>
<td>Fair time sharing</td>
<td>Context-switch overhead</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="process-creation">
<h2>Section 8: Process Creation and Control Primitives</h2>
<p>
        UNIX-like operating systems provide system calls
        for creating, replacing, waiting for, and
        terminating processes.
    </p>
<h3>Core System Calls</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>fork():</strong> Creates a child process.</li>
<li><strong>execve():</strong> Replaces the current process image with a new executable.</li>
<li><strong>wait():</strong> Allows a parent to collect a child's termination status.</li>
<li><strong>exit():</strong> Ends process execution.</li>
</ul>
<div class="code-example">
<pre><code>PARENT PROCESS
      |
      | fork()
      v
CHILD PROCESS
      |
      | execve()
      v
NEW PROGRAM IMAGE
      |
      | exit()
      v
CHILD TERMINATES
      |
      | Parent calls wait()
      v
EXIT STATUS COLLECTED</code></pre>
</div>
<h3>Orphan Process</h3>
<p>
        A child process whose parent has terminated
        before the child completes execution.
    </p>
<h3>Zombie Process</h3>
<p>
        A terminated process whose exit status
        has not yet been collected by its parent.
    </p>
<h3>Copy-On-Write (COW)</h3>
<p>
        Copy-On-Write delays copying memory pages
        until one of the processes modifies them,
        reducing unnecessary memory duplication.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="threads-multithreading">
<h2>Section 9: Threads and Multithreading Frameworks</h2>
<p>
        A thread is an execution unit within a process.
        Threads belonging to the same process share
        code, global data, heap memory, and open files.
    </p>
<p>
        Each thread maintains its own stack,
        program counter, and register state.
    </p>
<h3>Thread Mapping Models</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Model</th>
<th>Description</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Many-to-One</td>
<td>Multiple user threads map to one kernel thread.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>One-to-One</td>
<td>Each user thread maps to a separate kernel thread.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Many-to-Many</td>
<td>Many user threads share a set of kernel threads.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Threads vs. Processes</h3>
<p>
        Processes provide isolated address spaces,
        while threads support concurrent execution
        within a shared process environment.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="interprocess-communication">
<h2>Section 10: Inter-Process Communication (IPC)</h2>
<p>
        Inter-Process Communication allows processes
        to exchange data and coordinate operations
        despite having separate address spaces.
    </p>
<h3>Shared Memory</h3>
<p>
        Multiple processes access a common memory
        region. Synchronization is required to
        prevent conflicting updates.
    </p>
<h3>Message Passing</h3>
<p>
        Processes exchange information through
        send and receive operations.
    </p>
<h3>Pipes and FIFOs</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Anonymous Pipes:</strong> Commonly used between related processes.</li>
<li><strong>Named Pipes:</strong> Allow communication between unrelated processes.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Network Sockets</h3>
<p>
        Sockets support communication between
        processes on the same computer or
        across network-connected systems.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="process-synchronization">
<h2>Section 11: Process Synchronization and Critical Sections</h2>
<p>
        Concurrent processes may access shared
        resources simultaneously, creating race
        conditions and inconsistent results.
    </p>
<h3>Critical Section Requirements</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Mutual Exclusion:</strong> Prevent conflicting simultaneous access.</li>
<li><strong>Progress:</strong> Ensure eligible processes can enter a critical section.</li>
<li><strong>Bounded Waiting:</strong> Prevent indefinite postponement.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Mutex Locks</h3>
<p>
        A mutex allows one thread to own
        a protected resource at a time.
    </p>
<h3>Semaphores</h3>
<p>
        A semaphore uses atomic wait and signal
        operations to coordinate resource access.
    </p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Binary Semaphore:</strong> Represents two logical states.</li>
<li><strong>Counting Semaphore:</strong> Manages multiple resource instances.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Producer-Consumer Synchronization</h3>
<div class="code-example">
<pre><code>semaphore mutex = 1;
semaphore empty = BUFFER_SIZE;
semaphore full = 0;

Producer:
    produce_item()
    wait(empty)
    wait(mutex)
    insert_item()
    signal(mutex)
    signal(full)</code></pre>
</div>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="deadlocks-resource-management">
<h2>Section 12: Deadlocks, Starvation, and Resource Allocation</h2>
<p>
        A deadlock occurs when processes cannot
        continue because each is waiting for
        resources held by another.
    </p>
<h3>Four Coffman Conditions</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Mutual Exclusion:</strong> A resource is non-shareable.</li>
<li><strong>Hold and Wait:</strong> A process holds resources while requesting more.</li>
<li><strong>No Preemption:</strong> Resources cannot be forcibly removed.</li>
<li><strong>Circular Wait:</strong> Processes form a circular resource dependency.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Deadlock Handling</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Strategy</th>
<th>Description</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Prevention</td>
<td>Break at least one necessary condition.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Avoidance</td>
<td>Keep resource allocation in a safe state.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Detection and Recovery</td>
<td>Detect deadlocks and reclaim resources.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Ostrich Algorithm</td>
<td>Ignore rare deadlocks under an accepted risk assumption.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Banker's Algorithm</h3>
<p>
        The Banker's Algorithm evaluates resource
        requests to avoid unsafe allocation states.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="virtual-memory-paging">
<h2>Section 13: Virtual Memory and Paging Mechanics</h2>
<p>
        Virtual memory provides each process with
        an isolated address space, while the Memory
        Management Unit (MMU) translates virtual
        addresses into physical addresses.
    </p>
<h3>Paging</h3>
<p>
        Virtual memory is divided into fixed-size pages.
        Physical memory is divided into corresponding frames.
    </p>
<h3>Page Tables</h3>
<p>
        Page tables record the mappings between
        virtual pages and physical frames.
    </p>
<h3>Translation Lookaside Buffer (TLB)</h3>
<p>
        The TLB caches recently used address
        translations to reduce lookup overhead.
    </p>
<h3>Demand Paging</h3>
<p>
        Under demand paging, a memory page is loaded
        into RAM only when required.
    </p>
<h3>Page Fault Handling</h3>
<ol>
<li>The process accesses a page not currently in RAM.</li>
<li>The MMU generates a page fault.</li>
<li>The kernel locates the missing page.</li>
<li>The page is loaded into a physical frame.</li>
<li>The page table is updated.</li>
<li>The process resumes execution.</li>
</ol>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="context-switching">
<h2>Section 14: Context Switch Dynamics and Kernel Overhead</h2>
<p>
        A context switch occurs when the operating
        system saves the state of one process
        and restores another process's state.
    </p>
<h3>Context Switch Sequence</h3>
<ol>
<li>An interrupt or scheduling event occurs.</li>
<li>The kernel saves the current process registers.</li>
<li>Execution information is stored in the PCB.</li>
<li>The scheduler selects another ready process.</li>
<li>The kernel restores the selected process state.</li>
<li>Execution resumes.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Performance Overhead</h3>
<ul>
<li>Saving and restoring CPU registers.</li>
<li>Changes to cached memory translations.</li>
<li>Additional CPU cache misses.</li>
<li>Time spent executing scheduler operations.</li>
</ul>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="process-security">
<h2>Section 15: Security Architecture and Process Isolation</h2>
<p>
        Operating systems use hardware privilege levels
        and memory protections to prevent applications
        from accessing unauthorized resources.
    </p>
<h3>Hardware Protection Rings</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Ring 0:</strong> Highly privileged kernel execution.</li>
<li><strong>Ring 3:</strong> Restricted user application execution.</li>
<li><strong>Rings 1 and 2:</strong> Intermediate privilege levels available in some architectures.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Address Space Layout Randomization (ASLR)</h3>
<p>
        ASLR randomizes memory locations such as
        the stack, heap, and shared libraries to
        make certain memory attacks more difficult.
    </p>
<h3>User IDs and Access Permissions</h3>
<p>
        The operating system checks process credentials,
        user identities, and resource permissions
        before allowing protected operations.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="signals-events">
<h2>Section 16: Asynchronous Signals and Event-Driven Architecture</h2>
<p>
        Signals are asynchronous notifications
        sent to processes to report events,
        requests, or exceptional conditions.
    </p>
<h3>Common POSIX Signals</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Signal</th>
<th>Purpose</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>SIGINT</td>
<td>Interrupt request, commonly Ctrl+C.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>SIGKILL</td>
<td>Forced termination; cannot be caught or ignored.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>SIGSEGV</td>
<td>Invalid memory access notification.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>SIGCHLD</td>
<td>Notification of child process state changes.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Event-Driven Execution</h3>
<p>
        Event-driven systems respond to notifications
        or incoming events rather than repeatedly
        waiting for every operation to finish.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="containers-diagnostics">
<h2>Section 17: Containers and System Diagnostics</h2>
<h3>Operating System Containers</h3>
<p>
        Containers provide isolated execution environments
        while sharing the host operating system kernel.
        Examples include Docker and LXC.
    </p>
<h3>Linux Namespaces</h3>
<p>
        Namespaces isolate resources visible to processes,
        including process IDs, networking, mounts,
        and user contexts.
    </p>
<h3>Control Groups (cgroups)</h3>
<p>
        Control groups limit and monitor CPU, memory,
        and I/O resource usage.
    </p>
<h3>System Diagnostics</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Metric</th>
<th>Tools</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>CPU Saturation</td>
<td>top, htop, mpstat</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Memory Usage</td>
<td>ps aux, valgrind</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Disk I/O Wait</td>
<td>iostat, iotop</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>System Call Latency</td>
<td>strace, eBPF, perf</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Conclusion</h3>
<p>
        Programs provide the instructions that computers execute,
        while processes represent their active execution.
        Operating systems coordinate CPU scheduling, memory,
        inter-process communication, synchronization,
        security, and resource management to support
        stable multitasking environments.
    </p>
</section>
